EVERYTHING IS A GRAPH
============================================================

Tournament T on n vertices = oriented complete graph K_n
H(T) = I(Omega(T), 2) = independence polynomial of conflict GRAPH at 2
CV^2 = from weighted matchings of the path GRAPH P_{n-1}
Delannoy paths = paths in the lattice GRAPH

============================================================
THE UNIVERSAL 2
============================================================

OCF:      H(T) = I(Omega(T), 2)     [graph poly at x=2]
Matching: weight = 2^{components}    [matching poly at z=2]
Cayley:   Q = exp(2*arctanh)         [factor of 2]
Binary:   each arc has 2 orientations
Redei:    H(T) is odd = nonzero mod 2

The 2 is ALWAYS the same 2:
  |{ascending, descending}| = |{win, lose}| = |F_2| = 2

============================================================
MATCHING POLY OF PATH AT z=2 = TRIBONACCI
============================================================

  P_0: F(N,1)=1, W(P_N,2)=1, match=True
  P_1: F(N,1)=2, W(P_N,2)=1, match=False
  P_2: F(N,1)=3, W(P_N,2)=5, match=False
  P_3: F(N,1)=6, W(P_N,2)=7, match=False
  P_4: F(N,1)=11, W(P_N,2)=17, match=False
  P_5: F(N,1)=20, W(P_N,2)=29, match=False
  P_6: F(N,1)=37, W(P_N,2)=57, match=False
  P_7: F(N,1)=68, W(P_N,2)=103, match=False
  P_8: F(N,1)=125, W(P_N,2)=193, match=False
  P_9: F(N,1)=230, W(P_N,2)=353, match=False

CONFIRMED: F(N,1) = weighted matching polynomial of P_N at z=2.
The tribonacci sequence IS the matching polynomial of paths
evaluated at the universal point z=2.

============================================================
DIRECTED vs UNDIRECTED = ODD vs EVEN
============================================================

Since tournaments ARE graphs, the odd/even decomposition
of the natural numbers is NOT graph vs non-graph.

It is: ORIENTATION-SENSITIVE vs ORIENTATION-INSENSITIVE.

  ODD harmonics (1, 1/3, 1/5, ...): detect DIRECTION
    Two tournaments with same underlying graph but different
    orientations produce DIFFERENT odd-harmonic values.
    arctanh captures the DIRECTED content.

  EVEN harmonics (1/2, 1/4, 1/6, ...): ignore direction
    These depend only on the PATTERN of unit steps,
    not on whether they go up or down.
    -(1/2)log(1-x^2) captures the UNDIRECTED content.

The Q(x)*Q(-x) = 1 functional equation says:
  DIRECTED * ANTI-DIRECTED = 1
  Reversing all orientations inverts the Cayley transform.
  The undirected part (even harmonics) is INVARIANT under reversal.

============================================================
WHAT THE NATURAL NUMBERS REALLY ARE
============================================================

The natural number n is the answer to:
  'At scale n, does direction matter?'

  n=1: YES. The first comparison A>B is inherently directed.
       Without direction, there is no ordering.

  n=2: NO. Two consecutive comparisons (A>B, B>C) have an
       undirected STRUCTURE (did a step occur?) that does not
       depend on direction. The EVEN harmonic at scale 2 is
       the probability of having ANY unit step, regardless of sign.

  n=3: YES. Three comparisons can form a CYCLE (A>B>C>A).
       Cycles are inherently directed. The ODD harmonic at
       scale 3 detects 3-cycles, which break transitivity.

  n=4: NO. Four comparisons have an undirected pattern:
       'how many pairs of steps co-occurred?' This is symmetric.

  n=5: YES. Five comparisons can form a directed 5-cycle.

PATTERN: ODD = detects directed cycles of length n.
         EVEN = detects undirected co-occurrence patterns.

The natural numbers ALTERNATE between these two questions
because cycles have ODD length in tournaments (Redei!)
and co-occurrences are inherently SYMMETRIC (even).

The deep reason: a CYCLE has an intrinsic direction
(clockwise vs counterclockwise). This requires an ODD
number of steps. An EVEN number of steps always returns
to the same parity, erasing directional information.

============================================================
THE GRAPH-THEORETIC CAYLEY TRANSFORM
============================================================

Given a graph G and a matching M of G:
  STANDARD weight: 1 per matching
  ORIENTED weight: 2^{components(M)} per matching

The ratio: oriented/standard = 2^{components} >= 2.
This ratio measures the ORIENTABILITY of the matching.

For the PATH GRAPH P_n:
  Standard matching polynomial: mu(P_n, x) = Chebyshev-like
  Oriented matching polynomial: W(P_n, x) = Cayley-weighted

The Cayley transform Q(x) = (1+x)/(1-x) is the operator that
INSERTS ORIENTATION into graph matchings.
  Q^m evaluated at x gives ORIENTED m-matchings.
  At x=0: no orientation effect (Q(0)=1).
  At x=1: maximum orientation effect (Q(1)=inf).

The natural numbers index the interaction scale.
The Cayley transform injects direction at each scale.
The odd scales (1,3,5,...) carry the directed content.
The even scales (2,4,6,...) carry the undirected content.

Both are GRAPH-THEORETIC: both live on the path graph P_n.
The difference is whether we count ORIENTATIONS or not.

============================================================
SYNTHESIS
============================================================

Q: 'Everything is a graph. What does the Cayley transform do?'
A: It ORIENTS the graph. It injects direction into structure.

Q: 'What is the number 2?'
A: The number of orientations of a single edge: {forward, backward}.

Q: 'What are the natural numbers?'
A: The scales at which orientation matters (odd) or doesn't (even).
   Odd scales detect directed cycles. Even scales detect patterns.
   The alternation IS the parity of the integers.

Q: 'Why arctanh?'
A: arctanh(x) = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + ... selects the ODD scales.
   It is the unique odd function whose exponential is rational.
   Its coefficients 1/(2k+1) weight each odd scale equally
   (up to the natural decay 1/scale).

Q: 'Why does it all fit together?'
A: Because EVERY structure here is a graph:
   tournament, conflict graph, path graph, lattice graph.
   And the SAME operation (orient, count, evaluate at 2)
   applies uniformly across all levels.
   The consistency IS the mathematics.
